# Each claim carries the same wording into every statement that uses it, and its sense holds steady there.
# P4's "for those things that are greatest in truth are greatest in being" ties "truest" to "uttermost being". These are different claims, and the text gives the connection as a cited thesis (Metaph. ii).
# One candidate shift: "true" as a property of statements versus "true" as a property of being. P4 does not read as moving one word from one sense to the other; the text already uses "true" of beings in S2.
# I also considered whether "truest/best" in P2 means the highest among existing things or an absolute maximum standard. S3 already reads it as the maximum that things resemble, which is the same sense later statements use. So I mark no bridge.
